\f0\b0 \cf0 Participants will use Cloudinary to upload, manage, transform, optimize, and deliver images and video within their hackathon projects. Cloudinary provides a generous free tier for developers to leverage our APIs and SDKs that make it easy to add powerful media capabilities to web and mobile applications. Participants can use features such as image and video transformations, resizing, cropping, format and quality optimization, responsive delivery, and programmatic media management. Creative use of AI-powered media capabilities is also encouraged where relevant to the project. Check out the specific tools we\'92d like you to use for our challenge: https://cld.media/hackathons . To submit your challenge, you must fill in this form: https://cld.media/hackathon-survey .\
}}\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hackjunction.app/participate/45/hackerpacks/62"}}{\fldrslt \cf0 \
BY MODAL\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 $100 Modal Credits for CPUs and GPUs\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 What we\'92ll bring: Every participant gets $100 in Modal credits for CPUs and GPUs, up to the latest NVIDIA B300s, billed per second and scaling to zero when idle. Modal is Python-native: decorate a function, run modal run, and it executes in the cloud in seconds; modal deploy turns it into an autoscaling web endpoint with a public URL ready for your demo. Teams can serve open-weight LLMs, vision-language and speech models; run untrusted, LLM-generated code in isolated Sandboxes; fine-tune or RL post-train models on one GPU or a multi-node cluster with Modal Dojo, our open-source training library; and store weights and datasets in Volumes. We'll provide a Junction starter kit of ready-to-run examples. The kick-off workshop will be at the Modal booth and announced on Discord, and Modal engineers will be on site all weekend. List of technologies we are bringing: \'95 Modal compute credits ($100 per participant) for CPUs and GPUs: T4, L4, A10, L40S, A100 (40 and 80 GB), RTX PRO 6000, H100, H200, B200 and B300 \'95 Modal Python SDK and CLI (pip install modal), plus JavaScript and Go SDKs for Sandboxes \'95 Modal Sandboxes: secure, on-demand containers for agents, code interpreters and evals \'95 Web endpoints (FastAPI, WebSockets, WebRTC) with autoscaling and public URLs \'95 Volumes, Dicts and Queues for model weights, datasets and shared state \'95 Modal Dojo: open-source library for fine-tuning and RL post-training open models on Modal GPUs \'95 Starter examples: LLM serving with vLLM and SGLang, speech-to-text, text-to-speech, vision, RAG, agents and RL \'95 On-site mentors from Modal engineering and Developer Relations Extra details: Modal was built so that a single developer can do what used to take a platform team, which makes it a natural fit for a 48-hour hackathon. Best use of Modal is a tech challenge, so you compete in it on top of your main challenge: use Modal to power the project you're already building and you can enter. The same project can enter other tech challenges too.\
}}\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
